<chapter xml:id="portable_sql">
	<title>Portable Named-Function Dialect</title>

	<para>MobilityDB is used from several SQL engines: PostgreSQL through MobilityDB itself, DuckDB through MobilityDuck, Apache Spark through MobilitySpark and Apache Flink through MobilityFlink. PostgreSQL lets MobilityDB register operators such as <literal>&amp;&amp;</literal> or <literal>&lt;&lt;#</literal>, but the other engines cannot register all of them: DuckDB, for instance, reserves <literal>#</literal> for positional column references and accepts no operator containing it. A query written with operators therefore runs on PostgreSQL but not on every engine.</para>

	<para>For this reason every operator is also a named function, which reuses the operator's implementation and so returns exactly the same result, and a query written with named functions only runs unchanged on every engine. Since these functions form one dialect across the engines, each name must be free in all of them: a quantified comparison spells out its operation, as in <varname>eLessThan</varname>, because <varname>eLt</varname> would coincide with the function <varname>elt</varname> of Spark and Flink, and a topological or position test carries the prefix of the class it compares, as in <varname>spanLeft</varname> and <varname>stboxOverlaps</varname>, because <varname>left</varname> and <varname>right</varname> are functions of both engines and <varname>contains</varname> and <varname>overlaps</varname> are functions or reserved words of them. The spatial relationships (<varname>eIntersects</varname>, <varname>eDwithin</varname>, …), the restrictions (<varname>atTime</varname>, <varname>atValue</varname>, …) and the ordering comparator <varname>cmp(a, b)</varname>, which returns <literal>-1</literal>, <literal>0</literal> or <literal>1</literal>, have no operator and are named functions already.</para>

	<para>The sections below give the named function of every operator, grouped as the chapters of each type group them. An arithmetic or set operator takes the prefix of its operand's family: <varname>set</varname>, <varname>span</varname> or <varname>spanset</varname>, and <varname>t</varname> for a temporal value. A topological or position operator takes the prefix of the class it compares: <varname>set</varname>, <varname>span</varname> or <varname>spanset</varname> after the first operand that is not a base value; for a box or a temporal value the type of its bounding box, <varname>tbox</varname>, <varname>stbox</varname> or <varname>tpcbox</varname>, whatever the argument order, a temporal Boolean, text or JSONB value taking <varname>span</varname>, the time span that bounds it; and two base values compared with <literal>~=</literal> take the name of their type, as in <varname>cbufferSame</varname>. Thus <literal>t1.trip &amp;&amp; t2.trip</literal> is written <literal>stboxOverlaps(t1.trip, t2.trip)</literal>, the time position <literal>p1.period &lt;&lt;# p2.period</literal> is written <literal>spanBefore(p1.period, p2.period)</literal>, and the temporal comparison <literal>#=</literal> is written <varname>tEqual</varname>. The operators <literal>#@&gt;</literal> and <literal>&lt;@#</literal> are the containment of the time spans of two temporal JSONB values, or of one and a time span, the <literal>#</literal> marking time as in the time positions, since <literal>@&gt;</literal> and <literal>&lt;@</literal> between temporal JSONB values are the containment of their JSON values. <xref linkend="portable_sql_porting"/> states what else a query needs to run on Spark and Flink.</para>

